Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.240
welcome to my list of the top things&nbsp;
that python noobs do. i'm also giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.680
away four professional licenses to pycharm, so&nbsp;
comment #pycharm if you want a chance to win.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.720
some of these are actual issues for your code,&nbsp;
but a lot of them don't actually matter much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.000
nevertheless these things will tip people off to&nbsp;
your inexperience. so whether you're an actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.720
noob looking to get better, or if you just want to&nbsp;
catch any newbie habits that you still hold onto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.920
let's dive in. alright, newbie thing number&nbsp;
one: manual string formatting, aka putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.960
things together with the plus sign. instead, use&nbsp;
f strings. they're more readable, easier to write,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.000
and less prone to errors. number two: manually&nbsp;
closing a file. i can't tell you how many beginner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.240
tutorials recommend doing something like this.&nbsp;
open the file, write to it, and then call close.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.560
if this write call throws an exception,&nbsp;
the file will never be closed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.800
instead use a with statement, which will ensure&nbsp;
the file is closed even if an exception is thrown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.600
on a similar note, number three is using&nbsp;
try-finally instead of a context manager.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.680
i usually see this one from more experienced&nbsp;
developers but coming from a different language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.480
in python, most resources that need to&nbsp;
be closed have their own context manager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:13.280
use it. number four: using a bare except clause.&nbsp;
in python, keyboard interrupts and system exits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.320
are propagated using exceptions. that means, for&nbsp;
example, a bare accept is going to catch something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.520
like the user hitting ctrl-c. that's almost never&nbsp;
what you actually want to do. if you still want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.280
be lazy but you don't want to trap your user in a&nbsp;
box, then use an exit Exception, or if you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.000
do the right thing then catch the actual exception&nbsp;
that's going to be thrown. number five: thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.760
the carrot means exponentiation. nope, it's&nbsp;
bitwise xor. okay, that one's a really newbie one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.360
but i gotta pad the list somehow, right? number&nbsp;
six is: any use of default mutable arguments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.280
argument defaults are defined when the&nbsp;
function is defined, not when it's run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.680
in this case that means every call to the function&nbsp;
is sharing the same list, meaning the second time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.680
we call it, it's not starting out as the empty&nbsp;
list, it's starting out as the list containing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.280
zero from the previous call. probably not what you&nbsp;
wanted. if you want a mutable default, first set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.400
it to None and then check if it's None inside the&nbsp;
function, setting the default there. number seven:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.280
never using comprehensions, or if they do&nbsp;
use comprehensions, only list comprehensions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.280
a lot of code can be made both shorter&nbsp;
and clearer by using comprehensions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:22.800
you can have dictionary, list, set, and even&nbsp;
generator comprehensions. learn how to use them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.320
when they're appropriate. number eight: _always_&nbsp;
using comprehensions. i get it, you just learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.240
about comprehensions and now it's time to flex.&nbsp;
but stop for a moment, you don't need to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.520
every single loop into a comprehension. sometimes&nbsp;
this actually makes things less readable. i guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.720
readability is really in the eye of the beholder,&nbsp;
so you may not agree with this particular example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.000
but i hope you can agree that not every&nbsp;
loop should be turned into a comprehension.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.120
number nine: checking for a type with ==. there&nbsp;
are some rare cases where you do want to do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.800
but most of the time this is not what you want.&nbsp;
the reason is inheritance. a named tuple is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:07.360
tuple, so this Point class is a tuple. but it's&nbsp;
not literally the built-in tuple, it's a subclass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.920
in most cases you should program in a way where&nbsp;
you should be able to substitute a subclass for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.200
its parent. this is called the Liskov substitution&nbsp;
principle, and checking a type for equality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.400
is a violation of it. in most cases, what you&nbsp;
probably wanted instead was an isinstance check.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:29.120
number 10: using == to check for None, True, and&nbsp;
False. instead of equality, you should check for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.880
identity using the is comparison. this is what&nbsp;
== was going to do anyway, so just cut out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:41.120
middleman and use is directly. 11: using an&nbsp;
"if bool" or "if len" check. there's nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.000
particularly wrong about these. it's just that&nbsp;
they're usually equivalent to just a plain "if x"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.240
so using an "if bool" or "if length" check kind&nbsp;
of just shows that you don't know the language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.840
that well. number 12: using the "range length"&nbsp;
idiom. a lot of beginners, especially those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.880
coming from other languages, think about loops in&nbsp;
terms of indices. so they loop over the indices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.280
but only ever use them to grab out the elements.&nbsp;
instead, just loop over the underlying container&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.160
and get the elements directly. it's much easier&nbsp;
to read and less error prone. if you did actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:15.040
want to use the index though, you still shouldn't&nbsp;
use range length. use enumerate to get the index&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.920
and the element at the same time. another reason&nbsp;
i see people use this is to use "i" as kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.160
a synchronizing variable to get corresponding&nbsp;
elements from two different objects. of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:29.520
the better way to do that is using zip, and if&nbsp;
you still need the index use "enumerate zip".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.920
number 13: looping over the ".keys()" of a&nbsp;
dictionary. don't you know, that's the default.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.400
if you're modifying the dictionary as you're&nbsp;
looping over it, then it would be okay to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.520
a copy of the keys. depending on the situation&nbsp;
this might add a little bit of clarity, but even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:52.080
in this case the ".keys()" is unnecessary. number&nbsp;
14: not knowing about the dictionary items method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.920
if you're looping over the keys of a dictionary&nbsp;
and the first thing you do is grab the value out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.480
for each key, then what you really want is to loop&nbsp;
over the items of the dictionary, which are key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:06.320
value pairs. number 15: not using tuple unpacking.&nbsp;
do you have a tuple and want to get all of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.360
elements out as separate variables? well you're&nbsp;
in luck. that's exactly what tuple unpacking does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.240
number 16: creating your own index counter&nbsp;
variable. if you're starting at zero and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.680
adding one to something at the end of every loop,&nbsp;
then once again what you really want is enumerate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:27.120
number 17: using time.time to time. i think we&nbsp;
gotta give the noobs a break on this one. how are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.240
they supposed to know that time.time is not for&nbsp;
timing your code? time.time is for telling you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:38.320
what time it currently is and it's not as accurate&nbsp;
as using perf_counter. subtracting two subsequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:42.720
calls to perf_counter gives you the most accurate&nbsp;
way of measuring how much time it took your code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.240
to run. number 18: littering your code with print&nbsp;
statements instead of using the logging module.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.160
you can set up logging easily in your&nbsp;
main function with your own custom format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.240
you can also set the logging level or take it&nbsp;
as a parameter so you can filter out messages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:02.640
that you're not interested in. there, doesn't that&nbsp;
look a lot better? 19: using "shell=True" on any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:08.240
function in the subprocess library. "shell=True"&nbsp;
is the source of a lot of security problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.920
and ,let's be honest, the reason you probably did&nbsp;
this in the first place is to avoid putting your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:17.440
arguments into a list. number 20: doing math, or&nbsp;
pretty much any kind of data analysis, in python.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:23.200
learn to use numpy for array operations and learn&nbsp;
to use pandas for more general data analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:29.600
21: using "import *" outside of an interactive&nbsp;
session. "import *" usually litters your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.960
namespace with variables. instead, just import&nbsp;
the things you actually need. number 22:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:39.840
depending on a specific directory structure for&nbsp;
your project. a lot of beginner code assumes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.560
that all of your source files are going&nbsp;
to be in one flat source directory. they,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:47.840
probably unknowingly, are depending on&nbsp;
the fact that when you import something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:54.400
python checks for it in your system path. python&nbsp;
also adds the directory of the file being run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.120
to the path, so this usually works.&nbsp;
however, this can really get you into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.840
trouble if you have multiple scripts&nbsp;
that aren't in the same directory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.480
take the time to learn how to package your code&nbsp;
and install it into your current environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:13.840
number 23: the common misconception that python&nbsp;
is not compiled. have you ever seen those ".pyc"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:18.800
files next to your ".py" files? or maybe they&nbsp;
were in a "__pychache__" directory. those files&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:25.280
are compiled python code. but of course python is&nbsp;
also an interpreted language, so what's going on?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:29.680
well python is compiled, but it's not compiled&nbsp;
all the way down to machine code. instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:36.320
it's compiled to bytecode. that bytecode is then&nbsp;
run by the interpreter. number 24: not following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:41.600
pep8. pep8 is nothing more than a style guide,&nbsp;
it doesn't actually affect your code at runtime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:46.320
nevertheless, your co-workers, contributors, and&nbsp;
friends will nag at you incessantly until you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:50.800
conform. at this point, whether it actually&nbsp;
looks better or makes any difference at all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:56.560
is kind of irrelevant. experts do it this way to&nbsp;
avoid the nagging. and i saved the best for last,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:03.680
number 25: pretty much anything to do with python&nbsp;
2. python 2 hit its end of life years ago, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

